<h2 id="measure">Can Apple Watch measure cortisol?</h2>
<p><strong>No. Apple Watch does not directly measure cortisol.</strong> It records signals such as heart rate, heart rate variability and sleep that apps can use to describe stress and recovery patterns. A wearable wellness score is different from a laboratory cortisol result.</p>
<p>If you searched for “Apple Watch cortisol” or a cortisol tracker app, this distinction matters. Cortisol+ helps you explore wearable trends, but cannot tell you how much cortisol is in your blood, saliva or urine.</p>
<p><strong>Want to see those signals as a daily stress score?</strong> <a href="https://apps.apple.com/app/id6759510126">Get Cortisol+ for iPhone and Apple Watch</a> (free to download; analysis needs a subscription or eligible trial), or <a href="/compare/apple-watch-stress-apps/">compare five Apple Watch stress apps</a> first.</p>

<h2 id="track">Does Apple Watch track cortisol?</h2>
<p>No. Apple Watch does not track cortisol, and Apple Health has no cortisol reading from the Watch. What it can track over time is heart rate, HRV and sleep. Those trends describe how your body is responding, not how much cortisol you have.</p>

<h2 id="signals">What the Watch records, and what it cannot establish</h2>
<div class="signals not-prose">
  <section class="signal" aria-labelledby="sig-hr">
    <h3 id="sig-hr">Heart rate</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>What is recorded</dt><dd>Beats per minute; background samples and workout measurements</dd>
      <dt>Possible use</dt><dd>See activity and resting patterns</dd>
      <dt>Limit</dt><dd>A faster pulse has many causes</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>
  <section class="signal" aria-labelledby="sig-hrv">
    <h3 id="sig-hrv">HRV</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>What is recorded</dt><dd>SDNN, a measure of variation in beat intervals</dd>
      <dt>Possible use</dt><dd>Compare readings with your own history</dd>
      <dt>Limit</dt><dd>Not a cortisol concentration or diagnosis</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>
  <section class="signal" aria-labelledby="sig-sleep">
    <h3 id="sig-sleep">Sleep</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>What is recorded</dt><dd>Estimated time asleep and supported sleep stages</dd>
      <dt>Possible use</dt><dd>Review sleep alongside daytime wellbeing</dd>
      <dt>Limit</dt><dd>Does not establish why you slept poorly</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>
  <section class="signal" aria-labelledby="sig-temp">
    <h3 id="sig-temp">Wrist temperature</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>What is recorded</dt><dd>Overnight changes on supported models</dd>
      <dt>Possible use</dt><dd>Additional context for changes from baseline</dd>
      <dt>Limit</dt><dd>Availability depends on model and settings; not a cortisol sensor</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>
</div>
<p>Apple explains <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/120277">heart rate and HRV measurement</a>, <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/108906">sleep tracking</a> and <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/102674">nightly wrist temperature</a>. Follow the instructions for your particular Watch and software version.</p>

<h2 id="check-readings">How do I check cortisol levels on Apple Watch?</h2>
<p><strong>You can’t check cortisol levels on Apple Watch. There is no cortisol reading to open.</strong> To explore the wearable signals used by stress apps, review your heart-rate, HRV and sleep records instead. Keep those observations separate from a hormone-test result.</p>
<ol><li>On your iPhone, open Health and search for Heart Rate Variability. Review the available samples and their timestamps.</li><li>Review Resting Heart Rate and Sleep over comparable dates. Check which device supplied the data.</li><li>If a reading is missing, use the <a href="/blog/apple-watch-hrv-not-updating/">HRV troubleshooting checklist</a> before interpreting an app score.</li><li>For an organized view, explore <a href="/cortisol/track/#cortisol-tracking-app">what a cortisol tracking app does</a> and inspect its underlying inputs.</li></ol>
<p><a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/120277">Apple's heart-rate documentation</a> explains the optical sensor and model-dependent features. Those readings are not a hormone assay; <a href="https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/cortisol-test/">cortisol testing uses a biological sample</a>.</p>

<h2 id="cortisol-tracker">Is there a cortisol tracker for Apple Watch?</h2>
<p>Not one that measures the hormone. Apps sold as a cortisol tracker for Apple Watch, including Cortisol+, read your Watch data from Apple Health and calculate a score. That score is an interpretation of HRV, heart rate and sleep, not a cortisol level.</p>
<p>A useful cortisol tracker app should show which inputs it used and admit what it cannot know. See the <a href="#trust">three questions to ask</a> before trusting one.</p>

<h2>Does Apple Watch have a stress score?</h2>
<p>Apple's Mindfulness features support breathing, reflection and recording your State of Mind. A mood check-in is your own report, while a third-party stress score is an interpretation of sensor data. These describe different things and may not agree.</p>
<p>Cortisol+ combines available signals into its own wellness score. Other apps use different inputs and formulas, so a 60 in one app should not be assumed to mean the same thing as a 60 in another.</p>
<h2 id="start">How do I start tracking stress with Apple Watch?</h2>
<p>Begin with the recorded data. Check HRV and heart-rate history in Apple Health, then confirm that sleep is being recorded.</p>
<p>Add an app when you want those signals organized into trends or a summary. In Cortisol+, the useful next step is to inspect the score's input breakdown and compare it with how you felt that day.</p>
<p><a href="https://apps.apple.com/app/id6759510126">Download Cortisol+ for iPhone</a> to explore your permitted Apple Health data. Analysis requires a subscription or eligible trial. If you are comparing options, our <a href="/compare/apple-watch-stress-apps/">five-app guide</a> covers Cortisol+, Welltory, StressWatch, Bevel and Athlytic.</p>

