<p>Some labels in this older development build describe a person's recovery too confidently. Interpret them as software-generated labels, not established facts about your body. Our <a href="/how-it-works/">current methodology and limitations</a> explain that distinction.</p>
<h2>Why readings can be missing or change unexpectedly</h2>
<p>If your latest sample is missing, follow our <a href="/blog/apple-watch-hrv-not-updating/">Apple Watch HRV troubleshooting guide</a> to compare Apple Health timestamps, check app permissions and review watch settings.</p>
<p>Sampling frequency depends on the signal, Watch model and settings, and data must sync to Apple Health before the app can use it.</p>
<p>Movement, fit, charging, permissions and software settings can affect recording. Exercise, illness, alcohol, medication, breathing and time of day can also influence HRV and heart rate. These are reasons to check context, not assume every change represents psychological stress.</p>
<h2 id="testing">Wearable stress versus cortisol testing</h2>
<p><a href="https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/cortisol-test/">Clinical cortisol tests use blood, saliva or urine</a>, with timing and interpretation matched to the question a clinician is investigating. HRV cannot confirm or rule out a hormone disorder. Seek care for persistent or concerning symptoms rather than using a Watch score to decide whether a hormone test is necessary.</p>
<p>For a practical next step, use our <a href="/resources/stress-sleep-journal/">free observation journal</a>. For the underlying signal, read <a href="/hrv/apple-watch/">HRV on Apple Watch</a> and <a href="/hrv/normal-range/">HRV ranges and measurement methods</a>.</p>
<h2>Explore the questions on Perplexity</h2>
<p>We asked Perplexity about <a href="/resources/perplexity/#apple-watch-apps">Apple Watch cortisol-tracking apps</a> and <a href="/resources/perplexity/#hrv-sleep-stress">using HRV and sleep to understand stress patterns</a>. Read the shared conversations, exact prompts and our source notes. These are AI answers requested by the Cortisol+ team, not independent product reviews.</p>
<h2 id="trust">Three questions before trusting a cortisol tracker app</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Is this a sensor reading or a calculated score?</strong> Look for named inputs and units. A 0–100 wellness score is not a hormone concentration.</li><li><strong>Can you inspect the underlying data?</strong> Check timestamps and missing inputs before interpreting the summary. Start with our <a href="/blog/hrv-during-sleep/">overnight HRV checklist</a>.</li><li><strong>Does the explanation acknowledge uncertainty?</strong> A useful app should explain its assumptions and avoid turning a change in HRV into a diagnosis. <a href="/blog/alcohol-hrv-sleep/">Alcohol and overnight HRV</a> is one example where context matters.</li></ol>
<h2>Can an app calculate cortisol from HRV?</h2>
<p>An app can calculate an estimate or wellness score from HRV, but displaying a number does not establish that it accurately measures your cortisol. Cortisol+ has not established clinical accuracy against cortisol testing. Review the <a href="/how-it-works/">methodology</a> before interpreting its results.</p>
<h2>Which Apple Watch stress app should I choose?</h2>
<p>Compare the data explanation, permissions and subscription offer, not just the score's color. Our <a href="/blog/welltory-vs-cortisol-plus/">Welltory versus Cortisol+ comparison</a> explains two approaches and discloses that we publish it. For a wider view, see the <a href="/compare/apple-watch-stress-apps/">Apple Watch stress-app comparison</a>.</p>
